rated  because they aren't as good as they  would like to be  uh you know and and i was speaking to a  chinese  you know a lady who teaches chinese but  we were speaking in english and she was  saying you know she lives in thailand  and when she's together with a group of  thai people and they're all speaking  very quickly then she doesn't understand  very well even though she's quite fluent  in thai  and and i said well that's normal you  know you shouldn't have the expectation  that you can you're not going to be in  on every little thing that they're doing  that's they've lived with the language  since they were small they have things  they talk about references expressions  and stuff that you're not going to pick  up on so that's normal one shouldn't  feel  unhappy and i i see that quite a lot  people who have expectations of  themselves  uh you know i lived in japan for nine  years i would say for the first three  four five years it was difficult for me  to understand japanese sitcoms because  that's a japanese environment a context  that i'm not familiar with i could have  business conversations one-on-one or i  could even be with a group of japanese  business people who were talking about  lumber or economic things and i  understood it all but in that other  context i was not able to do it all and  and so i shouldn't have the expectation  that i can just jump in there and and  be comfortable if i wanted to be better  at that then i'd have to spend a lot of  time  watching japanese sitcoms  so that i would become more and more  familiar with that context  so i think uh you know even in terms of  you know usage  you're going to have mistakes  and um  i guess it depends on what the what the  demands are if you work for a large  corporation let's say in germany or in  in the us or in france or in japan  then i think the expectations are quite  high because you represent that company  you know as a foreigner i mean if you  are that is to say if you are a person  living in japan your expectations for  japanese or for german in germany or for  english in north america would be quite  high but if you are a japanese person or  a german person or a french person and  you're communicating in english with  someone  and that i've often had that experience  then i think the expectations are not so  great you can have mistakes it doesn't  really matter  so another part of managing expectations  is so first of all don't beat up on  yourself because any negative any  frustration is just going to delay your  learning is going to make it less fun  for you you're going to spend less time  at it  it never bothers me when i don't  understand these podcasts that i'm  listening to i know that i'm gradually  getting better  so i don't get upset at myself so i'm  managing my expectations  and your expectations have to relate to  the  goal like what are you going to use the  language for i'm sitting here in  vancouver by the way behind me is the  ocean  i just went for a swim it's getting a  little nippy now you know the  temperature of the water was 15 degrees  celsius 15 something i looked it up sort  of like 60 degrees  uh in the summer it goes up to like 64  65 which is closer to 18 degrees celsius  which is comfortable uh cool but  comfortable  and it gets warmer as you swim  when it's uh 15 degrees  it doesn't get warmer as you swim you  get colder  but you feel refreshed afterwards  uh but anyway leaving that aside  uh so that the other part of managing  expectations is  you know you should have expectations of  yourself in terms of your activities  now different people are going to  progress at different rates some might  be better in pronunciation some might be  better in acquiring vocabulary maybe  they read more widely or or whatever but  what you can control is your level of  activity that's why this 90-day  challenge i'm telling myself like i'm  trying to i'm trying i'm not always  meeting it but i'm trying to save a  hundred links that is to say on link i  save 100 new words  and i move 100 words to known like i  want to end up with 100 words that have  moved from you know words that i have  saved that are in my database to where  they're known  what i'm doing for those of you who are  on link what i do to make that happen is  as i'm reading on link i try to move my  words along to status three status three  is close to gnome  so they go from one when you first find  them they're one later on you might move  them to two they get a little lighter  yellow then i move them to three and  they're even lighter yellow and so if i  move all more and more words to the  status three then i can go to my  vocabulary section  i can sort for just status three words  and i can even you know and i go in  there and i start going through them and  i find that a lot of the status three  words now i know and so i move them to  note  and i don't worry if i move it to no one  and then later on i don't know it again  i move it back it doesn't matter but the  net effect of all of that is that i'm  reviewing these words that i'm close to  being able to say that i know  i'm trying to get a hundred of them so  i'm controlling my activity  and if i  think i know it today and i've forgotten  it tomorrow that again doesn't bother me  i don't have the expectation that  everything that i learn i will learn  forever  my expectation is that whatever i learn  a good portion or some of that i'm going  to forget again and and relearn so  that's fine too i accept that as as part  of you know my expectation the other  thing you can do in terms of uh  you know your expectations your  activities  i always quote this lady i can't  remember her name but she was the head  of the language department at the  university of san diego whom i met at  this  uh  american society  teaching a foreign languages or whatever  and she said there's only three things  that matter in language learning the  attitude of the learner the time spent  with the language so if you read a  grammar explanation in english that's  not so good if you're listening in the  language if you're speaking in the  language so time spent with the language  and then this business of noticing so  you have to want to notice so my father  for example who came from czechoslovakia  i moved to canada lived here for 30 odd  years spoke english very very well but  he had certain expressions that he would  never change like he would say  play it by the ear  it's play it by ear in english he would  play it by his ear like that was uh to  him and he never really got the th  and play it by the ear he got used to  saying that  and uh you know he would always say ward  and work instead of word and work  now if you're noticing what's happening  in the language  if you notice things  you have a chance of improving that  doesn't mean i i don't blame my father  he was comfortable he did business he  was fluent he was he was the best in  scrabble he had a tremendous vocabulary  and it didn't bother him that there were  these things that were not  say close to  you know normal usage so that was fine  but if you are concerned about that then  you have to develop the habit of  noticing things you have to spend the  time  you have to be positive therefore don't  beat yourself up when you don't quite do  or you aren't quite as good as you would  like to be have realistic expectations  control your activities  and try to notice what's happening in  the language and if you do all of those  things  then you can manage your expectations i  guess i went on a little bit longer  today anyway bye for now  [Music]

